WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Our next guest
is an award-winning,

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.136 size:84% position:42%
best-selling author.
His fantastic new book,

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:06.339 align:start size:94% position:6%
"A Swim in a Pond in the Rain,
in which four Russians

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:09.476
give a master class on writing
reading, and life, is out now.

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:10.677 line:92%
Please welcome back to the sho

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:13.346 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of my all-time favorites
George Saunders.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.148 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hello, George.
-Hey, Seth.

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:17.217 align:start size:94% position:6%
How are you doing?
-I am doing wonderful.

00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:20.420
This is a fascinating book
because it's a collection

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
of short stories from Russia
authors that are combined

00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:29.596
basically with you
teaching a class on these works,

00:00:29.596 --> 00:00:31.698 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is something
that you've done

00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:33.666 align:start size:94% position:6%
for years
at Syracuse University.

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:37.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
What made you think that thi
would be the sort of book

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:39.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
that people would
want to get into?

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:42.475
-Yeah, it's not a natural
selling pitch, you know?

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:45.278 size:84% position:42%
A highly technical book
with tables and figures

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:46.880 size:84% position:42% line:92%
about some dead people.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.749
Really, you know, I had --
Our students at Syracuse,

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:52.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
we get about 700 application
a year,

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:53.620 line:92%
and we let 6 people come.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.923 align:start size:94% position:6%
So they're just off the charts
great writers.

00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:58.491 line:92%
And over the years, this class

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:01.428 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I teach about the Russian
has been my favorite.

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:03.563
And I think it's been, you know,
one that they like, too.

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:06.066
So it's really just kind of like
going back to the basics o

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:09.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
what is a story, you know,
how does it work?

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:10.870 line:92%
How do we get pulled into one?

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:11.771 line:92%
How do we get kicked out?

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:13.506 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the point is that
by reading these stories

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:15.775
and studying them closely,
they'll figure out how to,

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:18.378
you know, write the stuff
that only they could write

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:19.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it's kind of like
story morgue.

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:22.715 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, we take these things
and we just slow it down

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.451 line:92%
and try to see why they work

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:25.652 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I always say to
college students

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:28.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
when they ask me for advice --
one thing I always try to

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:31.224 align:start size:94% position:6%
impart on them is college is
sort of the last time

00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:33.593 line:92%
people give you time to read

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:37.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
And what this book helped me
realize as well

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:39.332 align:start size:94% position:6%
is you never read again
in your life

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:41.367 size:78% position:39%
with someone sort of
going along with you,

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.203 size:81% position:41%
helping you understand
the context of this,

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:47.207 size:84% position:42%
which is what this book
does so beautifully.

00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:49.509 line:92%
And another thing is you also --

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:50.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
the first time
you read these stories,

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:53.346
you didn't quite understan
the depth of them as well.

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:54.747 align:start size:94% position:6%
You tell
a really interesting story

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:55.748 line:92%
which one of your professors

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.651
the wonderful short-story writer
Tobias Wolff,

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:01.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
read this out loud,
and you sort of didn't kno

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:04.257 align:start size:94% position:6%
until you heard it out lou
how funny Chekhov was.

00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:06.826
-No, that's right, and I kind of
knew I wanted to be a writer

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:10.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I wasn't positive, and Tob
gave a reading in Syracuse

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:12.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
where, instead of
reading his own work,

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:14.300 align:start size:94% position:6%
he read these three storie
by Chekhov.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.136 line:92%
And the room was just on fire.

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:18.538 size:88% position:44%
It was so funny and so,
you know, it'd be funny,

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:20.273 size:78% position:39%
and the next minute,
you're almost crying.

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:22.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's when
I kind of decided

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:23.209 line:92%
I want to be a storywriter

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:26.579 align:start size:94% position:6%
And also, you know,
with this book, these Russians

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
we kind of think they're
philosophical, which they are.

00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:32.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
And a lot of people think
they're hard or inaccessible

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:34.220 align:start size:94% position:6%
But that reading by Toby
showed me

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.822
that they're really just stories
from the heart.

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:39.659
You know, they're about things
that we actually face in life.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:42.428 size:84% position:42%
And they're real frank
and they're real funny.

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:43.263 line:92%
So that's why I teach them

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:45.565 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they're sort of
essences of story.

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:49.235 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's interesting because,
of course, you read these book

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:49.969 line:92%
from a modern perspective,

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:55.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you don't have a ton
in common with the sort of lif

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:56.809 line:92%
these characters you're reading.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you do have a deep empathy
for them.

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:02.815
And is that one of the thing
you try to really press on

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:04.751 align:start size:94% position:6%
your young writers,
is how important it is

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:07.554 align:start size:94% position:6%
to feel what these character
are feeling?

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:10.256 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, I think that's
the whole beauty of the story.

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.324 line:92%
You know, like, in real life

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:13.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
you're walking down the street
and some guy walks by.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:16.229
And you quickly glance at hi
and you make some projection

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:19.065 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you like him or don't like
him, you dismiss him.

00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:20.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
But in the world of the story,
you know,

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:24.537
guided by somebody like Chekhov,
you turn around and you get to

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:27.974
go into that guy's head,
and you stay there for 15 pages.

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:29.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if you're reading
a great story,

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:31.277 align:start size:94% position:6%
you basically become
that person,

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:34.547
even if you, in real life,
you wouldn't have liked hi

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:35.982 align:start size:94% position:6%
or you would have been
bored by him.

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:38.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
So there's something
really lovely about that.

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.219 line:92%
You know, to be reminded, one,

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:42.088 align:start size:94% position:6%
that other people are
just as real as you are.

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:45.658 size:78% position:39%
And, two, that if you
could actually occupy

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:47.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
somebody's reality
for a few minutes,

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:50.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
you'd actually feel more tende
towards them.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think that's the great lesso
of literature,

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:55.735 size:88% position:44% line:92%
is life goes by so fast.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:58.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
But a story is almost like
in "The Matrix"

00:03:58.171 --> 00:03:59.005 size:78% position:39% line:92%
when time slows down.

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:01.307 size:81% position:41%
And you get to inhabit
somebody else and go,

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.476 line:92%
"Oh, this is how her mind works.

00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:06.546 size:84% position:42%
This is how this person
suddenly became angry."

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:08.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's not something
you get to do in real life

00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:10.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
But in stories,
it's like a scale model

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:11.584 line:92%
of the way that people live.

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:14.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think, in the end,
at least when I'm reading it

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:17.257
it makes me a little more tender
towards people

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:18.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then also
a little more alert

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:20.593
to the beauty of the world
just for, maybe, you know,

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:22.495 align:start size:94% position:6%
15 minutes afterwards
or something.

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:25.298 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Can you talk about
how stories enter --

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:29.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
I believe you said they sort o
enter our mind as a memory

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:31.971 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, I think neuroscientists
are saying that

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:35.675
when you read a good story
it's coming in basically

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:37.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
by the same route
as an actual memory.

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:39.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think it's stored in
a similar way, too.

00:04:39.946 --> 00:04:41.347 line:92%
So this means that basically

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:44.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you have an energetic
reading life of good stories

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:47.553
you're getting all these
ancillary lives, basically

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:49.355 align:start size:94% position:6%
lives that you don't have time
to live.

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:51.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you're also
kind of being reminded

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.160 size:78% position:39%
that things have been
other ways, you know?

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.495 line:92%
Things are other ways right no

00:04:55.495 --> 00:04:57.964
than the story
that we're telling in our minds.

00:04:57.964 --> 00:04:59.532 line:92%
So I think it's kind of like

00:04:59.532 --> 00:05:02.001 align:start size:94% position:6%
consciousness expanding,
you know?

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:04.003 line:92%
It gives you a chance to see -

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:07.140
Well, what it actually does is
it works against the delusio

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:11.077
that you're the most important
thing in the world, you know

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:13.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
The idea that
we all inherit from birth,

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:15.515 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is that
it's the "Seth Meyers" show.

00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:17.350 size:88% position:44%
Really, it is, you know,
that everybody out there

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.086 align:start size:94% position:6%
is just for your --
you know, for your amusement

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:22.021 align:start size:94% position:6%
We kind of intellectually
know that's not true,

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:24.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
but basically we live
as if it is true.

00:05:24.424 --> 00:05:26.125 line:92%
So when you read a good story,

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:29.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
you're kind of being lured out
of yourself.

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:31.664 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you're having
someone else's experience.

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:34.200 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if nothing else,
you snap back to yourself

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.468 size:75% position:38% line:92%
and go, "Oh, my God.

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:36.803 align:start size:94% position:6%
Everybody else is as real
as I am,

00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:40.740 size:84% position:42%
and they're also having
these powerful thoughts

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.542 size:84% position:42% line:92%
of their own identity."

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:45.478
-I think sometimes people woul
think this is more important

00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:48.548
in things like screenwriting
or writing for television.

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:54.854
But you make it clear that even,
you know, Tolstoy or Chekhov had

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:57.690
that pressure of convincin
people to stay with them

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.793 align:start size:94% position:6%
as readers from each sentenc
to the next.

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:01.627 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yes.

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:03.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
I got a great lesson one tim
from Bill Buford,

00:06:03.563 --> 00:06:06.432
who was the fiction editor o
"The New Yorker" at that time.

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:08.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I had given him this story
called "Sea Oak"

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:09.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I really liked
and I had worked on it

00:06:09.402 --> 00:06:11.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
for five or six years,
so I thought it was perfect.

00:06:11.571 --> 00:06:14.374
And he was cutting it up, and we
were moving things around.

00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:15.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
I got really insecure.

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:18.578
And, of course, at that moment
you go fishing for a compliment.

00:06:18.578 --> 00:06:20.113 line:92%
So I'm like, "You know, Bill

00:06:20.113 --> 00:06:21.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
what do you like
about the story?"

00:06:21.647 --> 00:06:23.950 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he just took
a real long beat, you know

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:28.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he said,
"Well, I read a sentence,

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:33.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I like it enough
to go on to the next one."

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:34.394 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And that was it.

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:35.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it's kind of
an amazing thing.

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:37.096 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, when you really
get down to it,

00:06:37.096 --> 00:06:39.232 align:start size:94% position:6%
whether you're telling stories
through screenplays

00:06:39.232 --> 00:06:43.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
or short stories or novels
that really is the whole game.

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:46.806
If a person gets to sentence
four and is offended enoug

00:06:46.806 --> 00:06:48.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
to check out of the story,
then you're done.

00:06:48.941 --> 00:06:51.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you're really just trying t
keep the person

00:06:51.144 --> 00:06:51.978 line:92%
moving through the sentences

00:06:51.978 --> 00:06:54.480 align:start size:94% position:6%
And what I found is all th
things that we learn in school

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, things that kind o
are buzzkills like theme

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
and character and three-ac
structure and all that,

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:01.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
that stuff is
all contained in that.

00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:03.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you can keep a person
reading for 20 pages,

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:05.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
I guarantee there will be
character development

00:07:05.792 --> 00:07:07.627 size:88% position:44%
and there will be plot
and there will be theme.

00:07:07.627 --> 00:07:10.163 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it's kind of
an amazingly simple

00:07:10.163 --> 00:07:11.964 line:92%
and yet infinitely complex way

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:14.867 align:start size:94% position:6%
to approach a story
when you're reading.

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:19.505
With my students, like, "Okay,
when did you buy into the story?

00:07:19.505 --> 00:07:20.573 size:84% position:42% line:92%
When did it offend you?

00:07:20.573 --> 00:07:21.908 align:start size:94% position:6%
When did you decide
you hated the story?"

00:07:21.908 --> 00:07:25.912
If you can bear down on that
you learn so much about storie

00:07:25.912 --> 00:07:27.547 align:start size:94% position:6%
but also about your own mind
you know?

00:07:27.547 --> 00:07:32.518
What is it that will make yo
suddenly disappear into a stor

00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:34.620 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's actually happenin
in front of you?

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.322 align:start size:94% position:6%
On the other hand,
what makes you throw a boo

00:07:36.322 --> 00:07:38.291
across the room
because it's so false, you know?

00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:41.594
But I think all of storytellin
is just in that little impulse

00:07:41.594 --> 00:07:43.062 align:start size:94% position:6%
of "Am I going to
keep going or not?"

00:07:43.062 --> 00:07:45.364
I talked to somebody who i
a podcaster the other day,

00:07:45.364 --> 00:07:48.401
and she said, "Well, you know,
for us, it's kind of simple.

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:53.306
We just don't want people
to hit stop," you know?

00:07:53.306 --> 00:07:56.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's the age-old problem
for people

00:07:56.809 --> 00:07:59.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
who are trying to hold
anyone's attention.

00:07:59.545 --> 00:08:02.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
I really -- I mean, I love
reading these stories.

00:08:02.348 --> 00:08:05.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously, you selected ones
that meant a lot,

00:08:05.151 --> 00:08:06.552 line:92%
and you were not wrong there

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:08.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it was so wonderful
to read it,

00:08:08.521 --> 00:08:10.957 align:start size:94% position:6%
knowing that I was going o
the journey with you.

00:08:10.957 --> 00:08:12.725 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thanks for writing it
and thanks for being here.

00:08:12.725 --> 00:08:13.893 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's always a pleasure
to see you, George.

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:16.329
-Oh, thank you for having me on,
Seth. Good to see you again.

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:19.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"A Swim in a Pond in the Rain
is out now.

